---
source: crates/qr-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: "stdout_of(qr().arg(\"--help\"))"
---
A fast, secure, portable QR code generator CLI.

Usage: qr [OPTIONS] [TEXT]
       qr <COMMAND>

Commands:
  completions  Print a shell completion script to stdout
  man          Print the man page (roff) to stdout
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [TEXT]
          Text to encode; quote it if it contains spaces. '-' reads stdin, which is also the default
          when stdin is not a terminal

Options:
  -i, --input <FILE>
          Read the data to encode from FILE (raw bytes); '-' reads stdin

  -o, --output <PATH|->
          Write the code to PATH ('-' for stdout, not /dev/stdout). Existing files are replaced
          atomically and keep their permissions; a symlink is replaced, not followed

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format [default: from the -o extension; else terminal when stdout is a terminal,
          otherwise text]

          Possible values:
          - terminal: Unicode half blocks, forced black on white with ANSI colours
          - text:     Unicode half blocks with no escape sequences
          - ascii:    '##' and spaces, pure ASCII
          - png:      PNG image (1-bit indexed, or RGBA when transparent)
          - svg:      Scalable Vector Graphics
          - jpeg:     JPEG image (quality 92; at least 4 pixels per module)
          - webp:     Lossless WebP image
          - pdf:      One-page vector PDF
          - eps:      Encapsulated PostScript

      --ec <LEVEL>
          Error correction level: L, M, Q or H
          
          [default: M]

      --qr-version <N>
          Smallest symbol version to use, 1 to 40 (larger data still grows it)

      --mode <MODE>
          Data mode: auto, numeric, alnum or byte
          
          [default: auto]

      --mask <N>
          Force mask pattern 0 to 7 instead of picking one automatically

      --margin <N>
          Quiet zone around the code, in modules (below 4 may not scan)
          
          [default: 4]

      --scale <N>
          Size of one module in pixels (PNG, JPEG, WebP, SVG) or points (PDF, EPS) [default: 10]

      --size <PX>
          Width and height of a PNG, JPEG or WebP image in pixels, with the largest whole module
          size that fits (vector formats are at most this size)

      --dpi <N>
          Pixel density to record in a PNG or JPEG file, in dots per inch

      --fg <COLOR>
          Colour of dark modules: #RGB, #RRGGBB, #RRGGBBAA or a CSS name [default: black]

      --bg <COLOR>
          Colour of light modules and the quiet zone [default: white]

      --transparent
          Leave the background unpainted (image formats only)

      --invert
          Swap dark and light modules (or --fg and --bg for images)

      --data-uri
          Print the image as a base64 data: URI instead of raw bytes [default format: svg]. With -o,
          the file holds the URI: use a .txt name

      --no-clobber
          Fail instead of replacing an existing -o file

      --json
          Print a JSON result object on stdout and JSON errors on stderr

      --keep-newline
          Keep a trailing newline read from stdin or a file

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour; NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE are honoured in auto

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour when writing to a terminal, unless the environment says not to
          - always: Always colour
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

  -q, --quiet
          Print errors only, no warnings

  -v, --verbose
          Also print what was encoded and how

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  qr 'https://example.com'     Show a QR code in the terminal
  echo hello | qr              Encode stdin (one trailing newline is dropped)
  qr 'hi' -o code.svg          Write an SVG file (format from the extension)
  qr 'hi' --size 512 -o c.png  Write a 512x512 PNG image
  qr 'hi' -f pdf -o - > c.pdf  Write a PDF to stdout
  qr 'hi' --data-uri --json    Print a JSON object with an SVG data: URI
  qr -- help                   Encode a word that is also a command name,
                               such as 'help', 'man' or 'completions'

Exit codes:
  0 ok, 1 internal error, 2 usage error, 3 data too long, 4 I/O error,
  5 no code found (decode), 6 verification failed
